t the same time  that that is the same time that the plea  bargain sort of took off in new england  right so you have this post-industrial  revolution situation where the working  classes are getting bigger and bigger  and bigger and bigger and up until 1830  you know plea bargaining was taboo there  was i mean you would see prosecutors  actually get prosecuted for  trying to make deals with defendants in  the way that they do now so it was  completely off the table and just wasn't  happening or if it was happening we  didn't know about it  then in massachusetts you see that  number tick up  very quickly by the 1850s it's 50  percent of all criminal cases are ending  in a a guilty plea  by the 1880s it's 88 percent right so  almost all  uh criminal cases are ending in a guilty  plea by the end of the 19th century in  massachusetts  and what's happening there is that  there's this this giant explosion of  working-class people right and at the  beginning in the 1830s the courts and  every other american institution is  working really really hard to break the  back of organized labor you've got  the first strikes are happening in the  1820s  and then the first federation of labor  unions happens in new york in 1834  and so the ruling classes are sort of  panicking and coming trying to figure  out you know how to how to fix this  whole thing first thing they start doing  is prosecuting groups of workers under  conspiracy statutes uh so you get big  groups of them at a time and say all  right you know you're going to get  together and try to get higher wages or  you know  shorter work days or something like that  and we're going to prosecute you for a  conspiracy  and it becomes pretty apparent  early on that that's that's not going to  work by the 1840s they've sort of  scrapped that whole idea because there's  just too many of them and  there's this the working classes are  getting too big and there's  working-class people for the first time  sitting on these criminal 