stinct that if we were  ever in such a situation and we felt  that we were innocent we would fight to  the end and demand a jury trial and so  on and so forth what are the incentives  that are there for those who are charged  to end up taking plea deals well it's  every incentive in the world isn't it  you know so so that's what we've decided  to do in the united states that's so  different from the rest of the world  here we've decided that we're going to  give every carrot and every stick to  prosecutors so they can end up with a  conviction somehow right  so um and this was all still sorting  itself out by the late 20th century  and then there was this supreme court  case called borden kercher versus hayes  where you have right down the road here  in lexington kentucky you've got  a 29 year old black horse transporter  who um gets picked up for trying to cash  a bad check worth 88 right so he writes  a bad check  prosecutor comes to him says well it  looks like you were in trouble 10 years  ago over something and so  what you're going to do is for this 88  dollar check you're going to take five  years in prison  and paul hayes says i don't want to take  five years in prison prosecutor says  well if you don't take five years in  prison  then i'm going to hit you with this  habitual offender charge and that  carries a mandatory penalty of life in  prison  and that  he's like i don't want to do life in  prison over an 88 check but that's the  kind of power the prosecutor has  we're still sort of sorting out whether  or not we wanted to do that  in that case paul hayes  didn't want to take the deal he went to  trial got convicted and got a life  sentence  and that case went all the way up to the  supreme court the supreme court at that  time in 1978 essentially says yeah you  know it's the prosecutor's job to  persuade a defendant to give up their  right to trial  and they can do that by hook or by crook  they can do whatever they want to get a  defendant to to to plead guilty and to  forego the as the prosecutor says the  inconvenience of the trial  um and so that case sort of throws the  floodgates open  uh for prosecutors to use anything and  everything that they want  so you know if you have um if you're  charged with murder let's say  you know the prosecutor can offer  carrots can say look you know we're  going to bust these charges down to a  wanton endangerment and you're going to  do six months probation because i just  don't want to take it to trial and i  don't think it's a very good case but i  need a conviction so you know take it or  leave it and you'd be hard-pressed to  say i'm going to leave that on the table  right conversely if a prosecutor wants  to  proceed as though it is a death penalty  case  and threaten a defendant with literal  death if they don't take uh a plea  bargain to let's say life without prison  they can do that too and in fact when  you look at the numbers one of the more  chilling statistics that i've got in my  book is that since the 1970s 75 percent  of the people on death row  could have avoided a death sentence by  taking the plea offer that the  prosecutor gave to them so in other  words the prosecutor has the power to  say you know if you don't take this deal  i'm going to put the death sentence on  the tables not on the table until you  take until you say yes or no but if you  make me go to trial well death penalty  is on the table so prosecutors just had  this enormous power  to alter the legal landscape in any  given case that in the united states  that they just don't have in other  countries  is it easier for wealthier defendants to  avoid taking plea deals in the sense  that all else being equal  a long trial costs more money  sure uh for for for reasons that i said  before it's long trials cost more money  but they're also you know uh more high  profile  right they're they're more visible to  the public uh the media pays more  attention to what's going on in these  high-profile 