her gun  that a prosecution that the prosecution  can put to a defendant's head to say  like listen you know if you don't take  this deal you're just going to stay  locked up there until we have a trial  and that could be two years from now  that could be three years from now so if  you can't post bail tough luck there you  sit in the county jail and these county  jails as i'm sure you know are  oftentimes not friendly places they're  so unfriendly in fact especially  metropolitan jails are so unfriendly  that you see defendants taking deals  where they end up going to prison just  because prisons are a little friendlier  than the county jail right for example  you see that a lot  but but basically it's it's this arsenal  of tools that the prosecution has  cash bail being one of them to make sure  that some that that every defendant gets  pressed into taking a deal of some kind  i had a client that i represented in a  civil rights case who insisted on his  innocence in uh in these criminal  charges that were levied against him way  out in rural kentucky  and  he sat there in jail waiting for his  trial to come up for three years well  the maximum sentence that he could have  come up with  that he he could have been subjected to  was only two years wow on the original  charges so he sits there in three years  waiting for his trial and then goes to  trial and no police show up and they  dismiss the charges you know um and and  so that is the kind of thing that for  people that insist on their right to  trial they're going to get punished for  it it's what we call the trial penalty  it's what happened to paul hayes it's  what's happened to countless clients  that i've had and it's what happens all  over america every single day in those  situations um i think this was an issue  with with donziger in fact  when the strictest penalty you could get  is less than the time that you've been  detained pre-trial  is there some recourse or remedy in  those cases  well you'd think there would be right  you'd think that and and that's that was  you know the case with my guy down in  eastern kentucky yeah um but the federal  courts are so hostile uh in most of the  country to the civil rights actions that  you're not very likely to get anyway and  especially since this is such a common  thing like for people to stay in jail  way longer than they should stay in jail  for one reason or another yeah um that  you know most most of those folks that  have been aggrieved by something like  that in the system  can't even find a civil lawyer to  represent them to to certain to  vindicate their rights certainly not in  rural parts of the country where this  happens all the time  and when they do find someone to  represent them the civil rights actions  tend to fall flat on their face because  you've got these  judges that are hostile to these kinds  of actions to begin with right  the book is pleading out how plea  bargaining creates a permanent criminal  class we've been speaking to the book's  author law professor dan cannon dan  thank you so much for your time thanks  so much for having me

